WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.210
Speaker 1: It's great to be joined on the
program today by Neil Valley, who is a lecturer

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:12.040
in sociology and Rutherford Foundation Research
Fellow at the University of Otago New Zealand.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:18.801
I hope Otago is the right pronunciation, Neil.
Though the latest book is Utilitarianism Neo

00:00:18.801 --> 00:00:22.810
Liberalism and the Production of Uselessness.
I really appreciate your time today.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.210
Speaker 2: Thanks, David. Thanks for having
me.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:33.070
Speaker 1: So maybe to start with utilitarianism,
I think many of us could intuit we're talking

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:39.620
about there is something futile about the
way we've organized much of our economies

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.050
and the world and society and culture. But
what what did you what do you mean by the

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.989
term?
Speaker 2: Yes, So by the term, it really

00:00:46.989 --> 00:00:55.059
came to me as in the process of writing the
book. The book initially started out as a

00:00:55.059 --> 00:01:01.690
series of protests against cuts to the humanities
division at my university. I originally started

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:08.939
thinking about it was really like the the
kind of futility uselessness of the humanities

00:01:08.939 --> 00:01:12.820
within the kind of contemporary university
structure, the way that it's constructed as

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.760
useless, that if you do humanities or arts,
you couldn't get a job, so on and so forth.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.759
But then I started and more I looked into
that, the more I started to realize that this

00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:26.960
idea of uselessness or futility kind of spread
like much further into everyday life in the

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:37.100
21st century. And so I got to a futility idealism,
basically, as is many of my many people might

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:43.030
have heard of the word utilitarianism. And
on a very basic level, utilitarianism is that

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:49.189
you maximize utility for the greatest good
of the greatest number. So each individual

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:54.560
and every decision they make, they maximize
their own utility. And this should lead to

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:00.259
a kind of majority happiness. Yeah, basically
my argument for utilitarianism is that we

00:02:00.259 --> 00:02:04.840
got to a point now where each individual is
trying to maximize their utility, not might

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:12.650
be by getting an education and getting further
training and getting into debt to start business

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:17.849
or so on and so forth. But in doing so, it
actually leads to the greatest unhappiness

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:22.420
of the greatest number because we're entirely
individualized, atomized. So it's a kind of

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:28.080
inversion of the utilitarian code. And this
is the state that I feel like we live in,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.100
that the more we maximize utility, the more
it leads to the kind of deconstruction of

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:38.260
the common common good.
Speaker 1: There is an inevitable sort of

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:46.129
interplay here between the concept of utilitarianism,
the capitalist systems that, to differing

00:02:46.129 --> 00:02:52.840
degrees, most modern what we might call Western
countries, although the term Western is maybe

00:02:52.840 --> 00:03:01.040
not the right one. Neoliberalism as well,
it's hard to get out of including the sort

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:07.840
of media culture and the way in which most
people are learning about the world, so to

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.980
speak. All of these things seem completely
interrelated. And maybe one of the things

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:21.180
that has changed or maybe has become evident
is that the preferences and path that one

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:27.519
increasingly takes is less self-directed than
we might imagine and really more prescribed

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:32.819
by all of those other systems that I mentioned.
Speaker 2: Yeah, that's a really interesting

00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:36.659
point, actually. And it's kind of one of the
great kind of contradictions of that kind

00:03:36.659 --> 00:03:42.870
of neoliberal period is that everything is
about, you know, personal responsibility,

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:48.820
self care, taking your self up by the bootstraps
and making the most of your own life. And

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:53.230
yeah, as you make the point is that actually
the decisions that we're making supposedly

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:59.099
as kind of free individuals are actually prescribed
to us, that there's a certain structure in

00:03:59.099 --> 00:04:04.519
place that forces us towards certain decisions.
So for instance, as a small example I talked

00:04:04.519 --> 00:04:10.470
about in university at the start, there is
that the way that universities are constructed,

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:15.959
particularly across the, you know, the global
north is is towards kind of getting a degree

00:04:15.959 --> 00:04:20.859
that can get you a job. So if that's kind
of rhetoric that comes out from, you know,

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:26.770
kind of public discourse and media and so
and so forth, most most individuals and looking

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:30.860
at universities through that lens. And so
they make decisions of what they study based

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:35.639
on and, you know, kind of get a job at the
end of it. That seems on the surface like

00:04:35.639 --> 00:04:41.800
an individual decision. Right. And yet it's
entirely prescribed within this kind of structure.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:47.610
And I think this is an effect of neo liberalism.
And I think more recent studies of neoliberalism

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:53.350
have started to see the initial studies of
neoliberalism are often about the kind of,

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:58.820
you know, the was all about the kind of free
market and a rollback of the states. Individuals

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:04.120
are kind of free to do whatever they wanted,
free to to operate highly liked in the market.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:08.289
But more and more we've come to realize of
some brilliant studies of neoliberalism is

00:05:08.289 --> 00:05:12.840
that actually neoliberalism isn't like the
rollback of the state. It's about a very different

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:18.120
form of state intervention. And the role of
the state has become to increase the market,

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:23.780
to protect the market from democratic decision
making. And then the early neoliberal thinkers

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:29.449
often talked about this at the Walter Lippmann
Colloquium in 1938, where this kind of first

00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:34.789
big meeting of the neoliberal intellectuals
in Europe, and they talked to by the liberal

00:05:34.789 --> 00:05:40.900
thinkers of the 19th century, as opposed to
a kind of new a new liberalism, a neoliberalism

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:46.480
for the 19th century, laissez faire kind of
free market liberal. They use the analogy

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:53.160
of a road, and they said if if there are no
rules on the road, eventually people will

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:58.380
just keep driving each other, no crash and
so on and so forth. So what they they saw

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:03.699
neoliberalism as a kind of highway code almost,
that you set the rules of the road and people

00:06:03.699 --> 00:06:08.050
can drive. When people are driving, they're
driving to where they want to go. They're

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:13.729
seemingly making individualized decision.
But there's this over arching structure that

00:06:13.729 --> 00:06:18.090
guides the way that they drive, and that's
how they envisage the market economy and the

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:22.870
neoliberal society as not everyone go for
their life, do whatever you want. It's that

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:27.699
there's an overall structure that guides people
in a certain way, but people still feel like

00:06:27.699 --> 00:06:33.069
they're making individual decisions, but it's
actually guided by a certain method, a certain

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:36.830
structure.
Speaker 1: When in the book you write about

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:44.240
the useless and repetitive behaviors that
can become a trap of neoliberalism. Absent

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:50.460
any context, what I would imagine there would
be one sort of story that fits that is you

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:56.750
go to school to get the job and then you take
out debt to get the car and the house and

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:01.169
the credit card and you get a slight raise.
So you take on a little bit more debt and

00:07:01.169 --> 00:07:04.800
it's sort of like this endless rinse, repeat.
And by the way, you're basically buying the

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.250
stuff that media messages tell you you want
or you should want or whatever the case may

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:14.250
be. Is that one of the mechanisms that you're
talking about, and what else did you mean

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.470
by it?
Speaker 2: Yeah, very much so. So that's certainly

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:24.889
a good example. And I you know, I work with
with lots of students who are kind of increasingly

00:07:24.889 --> 00:07:29.480
wondering what is the point what I'm doing
here. You know, they they look at their, for

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.400
instance, their parents generation, and they
might have gone to the university, particularly

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:38.979
say here in New Zealand, they would have gone
to university for free and also would have

00:07:38.979 --> 00:07:44.000
been given a scholarship. So they would have
been paid essentially to go to university.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:49.509
And now these these students are coming out.
They're paying massive fees to go to universities,

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:55.180
all getting into debt to do so. And then they
come out the other end in debt to a kind of

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:59.900
depleted job market where a house the house
market is absolutely crazy. You know, in New

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:05.550
Zealand, the average cost of the house at
the moment is $1,000,000. So, you know, kids

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:09.530
coming out with that much that they're not
going be able to afford to buy houses. So

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:14.050
there's this kind of sense that all the things
that we're supposed to do, all the things

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:20.930
that we have been kind of told, well, will
lead to a kind of good future for us are suddenly

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:24.830
crumbling. You know, if you go to university
and get a degree, you're supposed to come

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:28.659
out and get a steady job and be able to kind
of live, you know, be able to get a high speed,

00:08:28.659 --> 00:08:35.469
able to do all the things that we associate
in the kind of Western or global and the global

00:08:35.469 --> 00:08:42.190
north as kind of a a good life. Suddenly none
of these things are available and so many

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:45.440
people are in that and they get in more debt
and so on and so forth. And it's this kind

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.580
of endless cycle, and yet we're completely
trapped and doing the same things over and

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:56.029
over again. There are no no way. It seems
like there's very few ways to get out of of

00:08:56.029 --> 00:08:59.940
that trap of thinking, this is what I have
to do. I have to go to university, I have

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:04.550
to get through further training, I have to
do so on and so forth to live this good life

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:09.190
in the future. And the more we do it, the
more it seems like our collective economic

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:14.260
and social conditions get worse. And that's
the trap I call the utilitarian condition.

00:09:14.260 --> 00:09:18.860
We get stuck in this kind of endless trap.
Speaker 1: And there really aren't. I mean,

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:22.530
genuinely, there aren't that many ways to
completely get out of that, right? I mean,

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:27.019
when we think about them, there's there's
the idea of the total off-grid life where

00:09:27.019 --> 00:09:32.450
you essentially leave leave modern society.
As the case may be. You have to figure out

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:37.860
a way to meet your basic needs and to really
do that without touching the standard kind

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:41.551
of corners of the economy that you're talking
about. It's extraordinarily difficult. Another

00:09:41.551 --> 00:09:47.230
one would be, well, you do it in a hyper drive
way for a short period of time such that you

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:53.100
make enough money that then you could exit
in a sort of different way. I don't I mean,

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.530
it's genuinely quite difficult to not be part
of it at all.

00:09:56.530 --> 00:10:00.970
Speaker 2: Yeah, Yeah, very much so. And,
you know, in the introduction to the book,

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:09.070
I make a distinction between futility and
nihilism and I think. They're like the example

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:13.440
you use of, you know, going off grids and
so on and so forth. It's a sense that you're

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:17.740
right, like there are few options. The only
options we have are to try and escape from

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.649
it. A very few people can. Yeah, you might
be able to move off grids, but then you're

00:10:21.649 --> 00:10:27.860
kind of outside of society. Yes. You can't.
You can't influence. You can shift the way

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.850
the world runs. You just. And that, to me,
is a form of kind of almost like a passive

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:35.310
nihilism. It's like I'm accepting that this
world that we constructed is meaningless,

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:39.180
and I'm going to sit outside of it. Yes. And
then there's more kind of active forms of

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:45.329
nihilism. Like you sample, you use is that
you you go hard in, you try and invent something,

00:10:45.329 --> 00:10:50.790
you do it and so forth. But even even entrepreneurs,
I guess they kind of Silicon Valley entrepreneurs

00:10:50.790 --> 00:11:00.190
are are trying to exit from contemporary capitalism.
They're trying to get beyond the kind of mundane

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:05.519
kind of existence that the majority of people
have to test to live in. There's two forms

00:11:05.519 --> 00:11:11.459
of exit there, but those exits don't actually
do anything by changing the kind of fundamentals

00:11:11.459 --> 00:11:17.990
of that society that we're mostly stuck in.
And the reality for the vast majority of us,

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:24.060
no matter what kind of situation when we have
to, we only have the kind of structures of

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:29.320
society that are available to us and we have
to somehow make the best of it. And it seems

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:32.980
like that's getting harder and harder and
harder. And there is, of course, a kind of

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:39.480
generational politics in that, you know, increasingly
we're seeing that I, I kind of knew class

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:44.820
politics emerged in the 21st century of basically
people who own property, particularly housing

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:50.579
and people that don't. And that often falls
in a generational way. And for a younger and

00:11:50.579 --> 00:11:56.889
this book often I do say in the introduction
that this book is a kind of is a kind of requiem

00:11:56.889 --> 00:12:02.670
for a doomed youth in many ways that for it's
for a kind of younger generation of the world

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:07.110
that they're emerging into.
Speaker 1: And so it seems that there's the

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:12.040
question of how individuals can choose a different
path. But then there's another question of

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:20.060
how can society be changed such that the default
path is what changes? And it seems I mean,

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:24.120
are there any actions that can accomplish
both or is it really you have to decide I'm

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:28.389
going to be within it completely, but try
to change it or I will try to exit personally.

00:12:28.389 --> 00:12:35.490
Speaker 2: Yeah, I feel like the former is
our only real option. And so I make I kind

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:41.851
of I call in the book that we need we need
to turn this actually to one another. That

00:12:41.851 --> 00:12:49.070
actually, especially on the last, you know,
post 1989, post the fall of the Soviet Union,

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:55.860
I kind of there's been a kind of an inherent
sort of distrust in the last of collective

00:12:55.860 --> 00:13:00.959
ness of collectivity. I think particularly
probably up until the 2000, the debt crisis.

00:13:00.959 --> 00:13:05.829
And I think since then we've seen the return
of, you know, particularly in the U.S. and

00:13:05.829 --> 00:13:13.300
the U.K., parts of Europe, of kind of democratic
socialism, the idea of all of a kind of collective

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:18.660
subject to a collective political subject.
And then in the conclusion of the book, I

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:23.510
construct a kind of I guess it's a theoretical
idea, all this collective logic, and I call

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:30.400
it the futility of it. And it's it's it's
a kind of recipe for a development of the

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:36.970
precariat. So the precariat kind of describes
a whole group of people who exist in kind

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:41.190
of precarious lives, whether or not they're
working conditions or even just their living

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:47.440
conditions. And there's also these people
are, you know, the traditional idea of a union,

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:51.730
which I think is also extremely important.
And there is a resurgence of a union, that

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:54.810
there's something, you know, people at work
in the same conditions and they share the

00:13:54.810 --> 00:14:00.170
same lives in many ways, certainly the same
working life, the precariat, that's a more

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:06.240
desperate collective subject. It can be people
working in Uber drivers, but it can also include,

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:12.540
you know, casual lecturers, university people
who who don't who are living in kind of precarious

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:18.360
conditions. And for me, the futility, it is
like an extension of that. It includes the

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:24.600
people who live in precarious lives, are a
precarious situation, but also so many of

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:29.040
us who actually on the surface look like we're
in quite secure conditions still experience

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:34.920
this deep sense of futility of what we're
doing. And I feel like actually this shared

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:39.800
experience of futility across different scopes
of social life can actually be the thing that

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:45.420
can if we recognize it in one another, we
can use it as a way to to build a kind of

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:51.170
political, a collective political subject
that can actually confront the kind of the

00:14:51.170 --> 00:14:58.880
way the world works. If, if, if we accept,
if we see our if we see futility in one another

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:02.980
and we go, actually, I experience this form
of futility. And of course, some people experience

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:07.440
much more extreme forms of utility than others
in the same way that some people experience

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:13.800
much more extreme forms of precariousness.
But that doesn't necessarily mean we can somehow

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:18.610
recognize it in one another and try and realize
and I think part of the problem of contemporary

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:23.600
capitalism, and particularly its neoliberal
mutation, is that where we are expected to

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:29.540
see everything as a reflection of our failures.
Essentially, anything that doesn't work, anything

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:37.199
we end up in that we can't get a job because
we in some way are broken. We somehow we haven't

00:15:37.199 --> 00:15:44.139
and haven't done the right thing and we really
want one kind of pleading really in the book

00:15:44.139 --> 00:15:48.351
is to see that actually these experiences
of futility are not personal defects. They're

00:15:48.351 --> 00:15:54.769
completely logical experiences of the kind
of capitalist system in which in which we

00:15:54.769 --> 00:15:59.880
live today.
Speaker 1: The book is fascinating and slightly

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:03.740
depressing, but only in the way that gets
you to really think about things. The book

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:09.180
is Utilitarianism Neoliberalism and the Production
of Uselessness. We've been speaking with the

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:15.199
book's author, Neal Valley lecturer in Sociology
and Rutherford Foundation Research Fellow

00:16:15.199 --> 00:16:20.300
at the University of Otago, New Zealand. Really
appreciate your time and your insights today.

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:22.079
Speaker 2: Thanks so much, David.

